---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `place value   128  64  32  16   8   4   2   1
position        7   6   5   4   3   2   1   0`;

const Figure2 = `  1   0   1   1   0   1   1   0
128   0  32  16   0   4   2   0

128 + 32 + 16 + 4 + 2  =  182`;

const Figure3 = `182 MOD 2 = 0     ->  0        left with (182 - 0) / 2 = 91
 91 MOD 2 = 1     ->  10       left with (91 - 1) / 2  = 45
 45 MOD 2 = 1     ->  110      left with (45 - 1) / 2  = 22
 22 MOD 2 = 0     ->  0110     left with 11
 11 MOD 2 = 1     ->  10110    left with 5
  5 MOD 2 = 1     ->  110110   left with 2
  2 MOD 2 = 0     ->  0110110  left with 1
  1 MOD 2 = 1     -> 10110110  left with 0.  Stop.`;

const Figure4 = `01000001

  as a number      65
  as a character   A
  as a color       a very dark blue-ish value
  as a switch bank off on off off off off off on`;

const Task1 = `function toBinary(n) {
  let bits = "";
  let left = n;

  while (left > 0) {
    let bit = left % 2;
    bits = bit + bits;
    left = (left - bit) / 2;
  }

  if (bits === "") {
    bits = "0";
  }

  return bits;
}

let numbers = [5, 12, 64, 182, 255];

for (let i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
  console.log(numbers[i] + " -> " + toBinary(numbers[i]));
}
`;

const Task2 = `// Add this below toBinary, in the same file.

function toDecimal(bits) {
  let total = 0;
  let place = 1;

  for (let i = bits.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    if (bits[i] === "1") {
      total = total + place;
    }
    place = place * 2;
  }

  return total;
}

console.log("");
console.log("--- round trip ---");

for (let i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
  let there = toBinary(numbers[i]);
  let back = toDecimal(there);
  console.log(numbers[i] + " -> " + there + " -> " + back);
}
`;

const Task3 = `let mixed = ["1010", "111", "10000", "1", "1101"];

// Put these in order, smallest value first.
//
// You already have toDecimal. You already have a sort from 2.12.
//   Do not convert them by hand.
//
// Print each one with its decimal value beside it.
//
// Then answer these in comments:
//   a) "111" is 3 digits and "1010" is 4. Which is the bigger
//      number? Does counting digits always tell you which is
//      bigger, and what has to be true for that to work?
//   b) Now compare "0111" and "1010". Same length. What do you
//      do instead?
`;

const ExampleOutput = `5 -> 101
12 -> 1100
64 -> 1000000
182 -> 10110110
255 -> 11111111

--- round trip ---
5 -> 101 -> 5
12 -> 1100 -> 12
64 -> 1000000 -> 64
182 -> 10110110 -> 182
255 -> 11111111 -> 255`;

const NotationJs = `function toBinary(n) {
  let bits = "";
  let left = n;

  while (left > 0) {
    let bit = left % 2;
    bits = bit + bits;
    left = (left - bit) / 2;
  }

  return bits;
}`;

const NotationPseudo = `PROCEDURE bitsOf(n)
{
  bits ← []
  left ← n

  REPEAT UNTIL(left = 0)
  {
    bit ← left MOD 2
    INSERT(bits, 1, bit)
    left ← (left - bit) / 2
  }

  RETURN(bits)
}`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 2.14: Binary Numbers and What Bits Represent</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 2.14</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Binary Numbers and What Bits Represent</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Place Value in Base 2</p>
              <p>Converting Both Ways</p>
              <p>The Same Bits, Different Meanings</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>You Have Met Bits Already</h2>
              <p>Activity 1.2: eight switches, 256 combinations.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Today you calculate with them instead of counting them.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Base 10 Works the Same Way</h2>
              <p><b>4,271</b> is 4 thousands, 2 hundreds, 7 tens, 1 one.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Each position is worth <b>ten to the power of its position</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Base 2 changes the ten. Nothing else.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Only Table You Need</h2>
              <Code code={Figure1} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Place Values" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Positions count from <b>0</b>, on the right.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Each is the base raised to the power of its position.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Binary to Decimal</h2>
              <Code code={Figure2} frame="code" lang="txt" title="10110110" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">A digit's value is the bit times its place value.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Add up the ones. Ignore the zeros.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Decimal to Binary</h2>
              <p>The other direction needs an operator you met in week two.</p>
              <Code code={Figure3} frame="code" lang="txt" title="182 to Binary" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Read That Again</h2>
              <p><code>MOD 2</code> gives you the bit.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Subtract it and halve, and you have the next one.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Subtract the remainder, then divide. <b>Third time this course.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Comparing Binary Numbers</h2>
              <p>Which is bigger: <code>111</code> or <code>1010</code>?</p>
              <p class="fragment">7 and 10. <b>The longer one wins</b> &mdash; when there are no leading zeros.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Same length? Compare left to right, first difference decides.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Now the Interesting Part</h2>
              <Code code={Figure4} frame="code" lang="txt" title="One Byte, Four Readings" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Nothing in those eight bits says which one is correct.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Bits Have No Meaning of Their Own</h2>
              <p><b>The same sequence of bits represents different types of data in different contexts.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">The context comes from the program, not the bits.</p>
              <p class="fragment">That is why opening a photo as text gives you garbage.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Grouping Bits Is Abstraction</h2>
              <p>Eight bits become a number. Numbers become a character. Three numbers become a color.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Each layer lets you stop thinking about the one below it.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Same idea as 2.5, one level further down.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>An Aside: Hex</h2>
              <p><code>#3B82F6</code> is three bytes written in <b>base 16</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Four bits per digit, so one byte is exactly two digits.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Not on the exam.</b> Useful for your own sketches, so it is worth five minutes.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Calculating the binary equivalent of a decimal number, and the reverse</li>
                <li>Explaining place value in base 2 in terms of the base and the position</li>
                <li>Comparing and ordering binary numbers</li>
                <li>Explaining why the same bits can represent different types of data</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Bit</dt>
                  <dd>Short for binary digit. Either 0 or 1.</dd>
                  <dt>Byte</dt>
                  <dd>Eight bits.</dd>
                  <dt>Place value</dt>
                  <dd>The base raised to the power of the position, counting from 0 on the right.</dd>
                  <dt>Abstraction</dt>
                  <dd>Reducing complexity by focusing on the main idea and hiding what does not matter here.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Create a new <b>JavaScript program</b> named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">2-14-binary</b>.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Decimal to Binary</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Write the code below and run it.</li>
            <li>
              <b>Work out 12 by hand first</b>, using the table from the slides,
              and check it against what the program says.
            </li>
            <li>
              In a comment: <code>64</code> comes out as a 1 followed by six
              zeros. Say why every power of 2 looks like that.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={Task1} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Binary" class="mt-3" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: And Back Again</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Add <code>toDecimal</code> and the round-trip loop.</li>
            <li>
              Every line should end with the number it started with.{" "}
              <b>If one does not, one of the two procedures is wrong</b> &mdash;
              and the round trip is how you found out.
            </li>
            <li>
              In a comment: <code>toDecimal</code> walks the string{" "}
              <b>backwards</b>. Why does it have to?
            </li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={Task2} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Binary" class="mt-3" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Put Them in Order</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Fill in the comment block.</li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={Task3} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Binary" class="mt-3" />

          <h5 class="mt-4">Challenge (optional)</h5>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Call <code>toBinary(0)</code>. The <code>while</code> loop never
              runs, which is why the <code>if</code> at the bottom exists. Then
              write <code>toHex(n)</code> for color codes &mdash; the same loop
              with <code>MOD 16</code>, and a way to print 10 through 15 as{" "}
              <code>A</code> through <code>F</code>.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Exam Notation -->
        <section id="exam-notation">
          <h2>Same Idea, Exam Notation</h2>

          <div class="row g-3 my-4">
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={NotationJs}
                frame="code"
                lang="javascript"
                title="JavaScript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={NotationPseudo}
                frame="code"
                lang="txt"
                title="Exam Notation"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            <b>The reference sheet has no way to build up a string</b>, so the
            exam-notation version collects the bits in a <b>list</b> instead.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <code>INSERT(bits, 1, bit)</code> shifts everything right and puts
              the new bit at position 1. That is how you add to the{" "}
              <b>front</b> of a list, which is what this loop needs.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code>APPEND</code> would put each bit at the <b>end</b> instead,
              and you would get the whole number backwards. Two operations, one
              character apart in effect, completely different answer.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b>Everything else is the same, and notice what did the work:</b>{" "}
            <code>MOD</code> and division. You met both in Activity 1.6, used
            them again for the binary-search midpoint at 2.10, and here they are
            a third time. <b>The reference sheet is small, so the same few
            operators keep coming back.</b>
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Tasks 1 and 2 together should produce exactly this.</p>

          <Code
            code={ExampleOutput}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            title="Example Output"
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Your Task 3 output will be five lines, in order, each with its decimal
            value.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>The round trip in Task 2 is a test, not a feature. Explain what it can catch that reading the code cannot.</li>
            <li><code>01000001</code> is the number 65 and the letter A and a color. What decides which one it is?</li>
            <li>Base 10 needs ten digits and base 2 needs two. Explain what a digit's position tells you, in a sentence that works for both.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
